Fsphere – Project Structure and Architecture

This document explains the organization of the Fsphere codebase, the responsibilities of its directories and files, and the architectural principles that guide development.

The project follows a modular structure that emphasizes separation of concerns, code reusability, maintainability, and centralized handling of shared operations.

1. Root Directory

The root directory contains the main application entry point and project-level documentation.

File/Folder| Responsibility
"signup.html"| Main signup page and current application entry point for registration.
"src/"| Contains application source code, assets, and stylesheets.
"docs/"| Contains project documentation.
"README.md"| Introduces the project and explains its purpose.
"CONTRIBUTING.md"| Provides guidelines for contributors.
"LICENSE"| Contains the MIT License.

2. Source Directory ("src/")

The "src" directory contains the application's implementation.

It is divided into four main areas:

- "images/"
- "pages/"
- "scripts/"
- "styles/"
- "utils/"

2.1 Images ("src/images/")

Contains image assets used throughout the application.

Current contents:

- "logo.png" – Fsphere application logo.

New application images and other visual assets should be organized here.

2.2 Pages ("src/pages/")

Contains HTML documents representing individual application pages.

Current contents:

- "signin.html" – Sign-in page interface.
- "email_verification.html" – Email verification page.

Each page should contain its HTML structure and references to the stylesheets and JavaScript modules responsible for its functionality.

2.3 Scripts ("src/scripts/")

Contains JavaScript modules responsible for application behavior, user interactions, and Firebase integration.

Firebase Configuration

"firebase_config.js"

Initializes Firebase and exports the application's Firebase Authentication and Cloud Firestore instances.

Shared Firebase instances should be imported from this module instead of repeatedly initializing Firebase in different files.

Signup ("src/scripts/signup/")

Contains the modules responsible for registration and email verification.

File| Responsibility
"signup-init.js"| Entry point that starts the signup initialization process.
"signup.js"| Coordinates signup initialization and connects UI events to authentication operations.
"signup_UI.js"| Renders the signup interface, handles password visibility, and manages loading states.
"create_account.js"| Handles email/password registration, user profile creation in Firestore, and verification email delivery.
"googleSignin.js"| Handles Google authentication through Firebase.
"email_verification.js"| Handles email verification status, verification checks, and resending verification emails.
"email_verification_UI.js"| Renders the email verification interface.

The signup modules are separated according to their responsibilities to avoid placing all application logic in one file.

2.4 Styles ("src/styles/")

Contains the CSS files responsible for application presentation.

Global Authentication Styles ("auth_global/")

- "global.css" – Shared global styling.
- "auth-header.css" – Shared authentication header styling.
- "auth-responsive.css" – Responsive styling for authentication interfaces.

Sign-in ("signin/")

- "signin.css" – Styling for the sign-in interface.

Signup ("signup/")

- "signup.css" – Styling for the signup interface.
- "emaiI_verification.css" – Styling for the email verification interface.

Other Stylesheets

- "loading.css" – Shared loading overlay, spinner, and error-card styling.

Shared styles should be maintained in appropriate shared stylesheets rather than unnecessarily duplicated across pages.

2.5 Utilities ("src/utils/")

Contains reusable functions that provide shared functionality across the application.

Current contents:

"authErrorHandler.js"

Centralizes Firebase authentication and related database error handling.

It examines error codes and determines the appropriate user-facing error message.

"authErrorDisplayer.js"

Responsible for displaying and hiding authentication error messages through the application's error card.

The separation allows error interpretation and error presentation to remain independent.

New reusable functionality should be placed in this directory when it serves multiple parts of the application.

3. Architectural Principles

Fsphere follows these development principles:

Separation of Concerns

Each module should have a clear responsibility.

For example, UI rendering, authentication operations, error handling, and Firebase initialization should not unnecessarily be mixed together.

Code Reusability

Common operations should be implemented once and reused wherever necessary.

Avoid repeating the same logic across different application modules.

Centralized Error Handling

Shared error interpretation and presentation should be handled through the appropriate utility modules.

Avoid creating separate authentication error handlers for individual pages when existing shared utilities can handle the operation.

Modular Development

JavaScript functionality should be divided into manageable modules with clear responsibilities.

Use ES module imports and exports to share functionality.

Maintainability

Write code that other developers can understand, maintain, debug, and extend without unnecessarily modifying unrelated parts of the application.

4. Adding New Features

When introducing new features:

1. Identify the responsibility of the feature.
2. Place its files in the appropriate directory.
3. Separate UI logic from application and data operations where appropriate.
4. Reuse existing utilities and shared functionality.
5. Avoid unnecessary duplication.
6. Keep related files organized in meaningful feature directories.

As Fsphere grows, its structure may evolve to accommodate additional features and responsibilities.

5. Development Status

This documentation describes the current project structure and architectural direction.

The application is under active development. New directories, modules, and architectural improvements may be introduced as development progresses.
